import type { Lesson } from "./lessons";

export const lesson013 = {
  number: 13,
  module: "Module 1: How a Chip Is Physically Made",
  title: "Chemical Mechanical Planarization — Why the Wafer Must Be Flattened Again and Again",
  date: "2026-08-19",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 008: Process control finds defects and measures critical dimensions early enough to protect yield and prevent bad wafers from absorbing more expensive processing.

Lesson 009: Yield converts technical process quality into economics because more good dies from the same wafer lowers effective chip cost.

Lesson 010: Ion implantation shoots dopant ions into silicon at controlled dose, depth and angle to create electrically useful regions.

Lesson 011: Annealing repairs implantation damage and electrically activates dopants while the fab carefully manages the wafer's thermal budget.

Lesson 012: Cleaning repeatedly resets the wafer's physical and chemical surface so particles, residues and drying damage do not poison later steps.

The big idea

A chip is built by repeatedly adding material and carving patterns into it. That naturally makes the wafer surface uneven.

But lithography and later material layers work best on an extremely flat surface.

Chemical mechanical planarization, or CMP, is the fab's way of flattening the wafer again and again while the chip is being built.

The simplest mental model is:

Uneven wafer → chemistry weakens or reacts with the unwanted high material → a polishing pad mechanically removes it → endpoint control stops at the right level → cleaning removes slurry and particles → the flat wafer continues to the next step.

CMP is therefore not ordinary polishing. It is nanometer-scale controlled material removal embedded throughout advanced semiconductor manufacturing.

1. Why does the wafer become uneven?

Imagine building a city by adding roads, buildings and insulation one layer at a time.

After several layers, the top surface would no longer be flat. Hills would form above existing structures and valleys between them.

A wafer behaves similarly.

Deposition adds films. Etch removes selected regions. Trenches are filled. Metal interconnects are formed. Each operation changes surface topography.

If the fab simply kept stacking new layers on top, the unevenness would accumulate.

That creates a major problem for lithography: the scanner needs the next pattern to be focused and aligned accurately across the wafer. Large height variation makes that harder.

So the fab periodically resets the surface to something close to flat.

That reset is CMP.

2. Why "chemical mechanical"?

CMP combines two mechanisms.

Chemical action changes or softens the wafer material at the surface.

Mechanical action removes that reacted material by pressing the wafer against a rotating polishing pad while slurry flows between them.

The slurry typically contains liquid chemistry plus tiny abrasive particles.

Think of the chemistry as making the target material easier to remove and the pad/abrasive system as physically carrying that material away.

Using both gives engineers more control than simply grinding the wafer.

The recipe can be tuned for removal rate, selectivity, uniformity, defects and the specific material being polished.

3. What physically happens inside a CMP tool?

A wafer is held face-down by a polishing head.

Below it is a rotating platen covered by a polishing pad. Slurry is delivered onto the pad. The wafer and pad move relative to one another while controlled pressure is applied.

Material is gradually removed from the high regions of the wafer.

Modern tools divide the polishing head into pressure zones so engineers can correct differences between the center and edge of a 300 mm wafer.

After polishing, the wafer must be cleaned and dried because slurry particles and removed material cannot be allowed to remain on the surface.

This directly connects CMP to yesterday's lesson: polishing creates contamination that the fab immediately has to remove.

4. The central challenge: stop at exactly the right place

CMP is destructive. Once material is polished away, the fab cannot simply put the exact structure back.

Remove too little and unwanted material remains.

Remove too much and useful structures can be thinned or damaged.

So CMP systems use endpoint detection and in-line process control to determine when the desired layer has been reached.

Applied Materials' Reflexion platform, for example, uses in-situ endpoint methods and profile control while the wafer is being polished.

This is another example of Lesson 008's process-control principle: advanced manufacturing increasingly combines the process tool with measurement and feedback rather than treating them as completely separate activities.

5. A concrete example: filling a trench

Suppose the fab etches trenches into an insulating layer and then deposits metal over the entire surface.

The metal fills the trenches, which is useful, but it also coats the top surface, which is not wanted.

CMP can polish away the excess metal until metal remains mainly inside the intended trenches.

The result is embedded wiring surrounded by insulating material with a much flatter top surface.

The fab can now build another layer on top.

This is why CMP became especially important as chips adopted complex multilayer copper interconnects.

6. CMP is repeated, not one-and-done

Modern chips contain many material layers, so planarization occurs repeatedly at different points in the flow.

CMP can be used for materials and structures involving dielectrics, tungsten, copper, polysilicon and shallow-trench isolation, among others.

The exact number of CMP steps varies substantially by device and process node, so there is no honest universal number to memorize.

What matters is the direction: more layers, new materials and more three-dimensional structures can create additional or more demanding planarization steps.

Applied Materials noted that some 3D architectures added as many as roughly ten polishing steps relative to earlier structures. Treat that as an architecture-specific illustration, not a rule for every advanced chip.

7. Why flatness becomes harder as chips get more complex

CMP has to remove material quickly enough for high-volume manufacturing while leaving an extraordinarily uniform surface.

EBARA describes advanced CMP as controlling flatness on a 300 mm wafer at roughly the 10-nanometer scale.

That scale comparison is worth remembering.

A wafer is 300 millimeters across, or 300 million nanometers. Yet the allowed surface variation can be measured in only tens of nanometers.

The tool therefore has to control a very large physical object relative to the tiny variation that matters electrically and lithographically.

This is one of the recurring patterns in semiconductor equipment: the economic value comes from controlling extremely small tolerances repeatedly at industrial throughput.

8. Throughput and the fab bottleneck question

CMP is necessary, but "necessary" does not automatically mean it is the fab's dominant bottleneck.

The operational question is whether enough CMP capacity exists to process all wafers at the required recipes without creating queues.

A CMP tool may contain several polishing stations, heads, cleaners and dryers so multiple operations can happen efficiently inside one platform.

Applied Materials says its Reflexion LK Prime architecture uses four polishing pads, six polishing heads, eight cleaning chambers and two drying chambers; its larger module count can provide up to roughly double the throughput of the prior LK configuration for some applications.

That tells you how equipment companies attack a capacity problem: not only by making the polishing chemistry faster, but by adding parallel stations, improving wafer handling and integrating cleaning.

If CMP capacity is short, wafers queue before planarization and cycle time rises. If CMP is poorly controlled, the bigger risk is yield: over-polish, scratches, non-uniformity or residue can damage expensive wafers.

So CMP can be both a throughput constraint and a yield constraint, depending on the fab and process.

9. The consumables layer: pads and slurry

CMP has an important economic feature that differs from many capital tools: every wafer consumes materials.

The polishing pad wears. Slurry is continuously used. Pad conditioners, filters and cleaning chemicals also matter.

That creates two economic layers:

Capital equipment — the CMP system itself.

Recurring consumables — slurry, pads and related materials required to keep processing wafers.

This distinction matters for investing. Equipment revenue can swing sharply with fab capital-spending cycles. Consumables can be more closely tied to actual wafer production because fabs need them while tools are running.

But consumables still face pricing, qualification and market-share competition. Recurring does not mean risk-free.

10. Applied Materials: the major equipment franchise

Applied Materials is one of the two companies you should immediately associate with CMP equipment.

Its Reflexion family is designed for high-volume 300 mm semiconductor production and integrates polishing, cleaning, drying, endpoint measurement and process control.

Applied's advantage is broader than the polishing pad itself. It can combine materials engineering, process recipes, control technology, cleaning and a large installed base.

That matters because CMP performance is a system problem: slurry chemistry, pad behavior, pressure, wafer profile, endpoint detection and cleaning all interact.

For Applied Materials as a whole, however, CMP is only one part of a much larger semiconductor-equipment portfolio. Do not confuse a strong CMP position with CMP being the majority of Applied's economics.

11. EBARA: the other major CMP name

EBARA is the second company to remember.

The Japanese industrial company says it has the world's No. 2 share in CMP systems and has shipped more than 4,000 CMP systems cumulatively.

Its F-REX family emphasizes high utilization, throughput and precise planarization.

EBARA is interesting because its semiconductor exposure also includes dry vacuum pumps, gas abatement and other fab infrastructure.

The competitive structure is therefore quite different from EUV lithography. CMP has strong incumbency, qualification and process know-how, but there is more than one credible large supplier.

That generally means less monopoly-like pricing power than ASML enjoys in EUV.

12. Why qualification matters

Suppose a fab has tuned a copper CMP recipe around a particular tool, pad, slurry, pressure profile and endpoint system.

Changing one component can alter removal rate, selectivity, scratches, dishing, erosion and wafer uniformity.

The fab then has to prove that the new combination works reliably across enormous wafer volumes.

That makes switching costly even when an alternative supplier exists.

This is a recurring moat in semiconductor manufacturing:

The tool does not need to be physically impossible to copy. It can still be sticky because the customer's entire manufacturing recipe has been qualified around it.

13. Logic versus DRAM versus NAND

CMP matters across several device types, but the job differs.

Leading-edge logic uses CMP repeatedly in transistor formation and multilayer interconnect construction, where tight planarity and defect control support later lithography and wiring.

DRAM uses CMP in structures that must remain extremely uniform as memory cells and interconnects scale.

3D NAND creates a different challenge: extremely tall stacks and broad topography can require long, stable polishing steps. Applied specifically highlights thicker films and broad topography in 3D NAND as reasons for multi-platen polishing strategies.

So "CMP demand" is not one homogeneous market. Tool configuration and process intensity depend on what the fab is building.

14. Macro scale intuition: turn wafer capacity into tool demand

Here is the quantitative habit to start building.

When someone says a fab is adding 50,000 wafer starts per month, do not stop at the wafer number.

Ask:

How many CMP steps does each wafer require?

How long does each recipe occupy a polishing station?

How many wafers per hour can the installed CMP fleet process at realistic uptime?

How much redundancy does the fab need?

That translation turns semiconductor demand into equipment demand.

A simplified example: if 50,000 wafers per month each require 20 CMP passes, the fab must support about 1 million CMP wafer-passes per month. If a hypothetical effective CMP capacity were 60 wafer-passes per hour per tool after recipe mix and uptime, one continuously available tool would provide roughly 43,000 passes in a 30-day month. Dividing 1 million by 43,000 suggests roughly 23 tool-equivalents before allowing for maintenance, redundancy, scheduling and process-specific constraints.

Those numbers are illustrative assumptions, not an estimate for a real fab. The skill is the translation:

Wafer starts × process steps ÷ effective tool throughput = rough tool-capacity requirement.

We will reuse that equation throughout the course.

15. Is CMP a major AI bottleneck?

Usually not in the same way that leading-edge lithography capacity, HBM, advanced packaging or power can become headline AI constraints.

CMP is better thought of as an enabling process that can become locally constraining if a fab lacks enough qualified capacity or if a difficult new structure creates a yield problem.

That distinction matters.

A process can be indispensable without being the scarcest resource in the system.

For an investor, "AI chips require CMP" is therefore too weak a thesis by itself.

A better thesis would require evidence that AI-driven device architectures are increasing CMP steps or difficulty enough to create disproportionate tool or consumables growth, and that a particular supplier captures that growth with durable share and attractive economics.

16. How CMP connects the fab flow so far

You can now see a larger manufacturing loop:

Deposition adds material.

Lithography defines where structures should exist.

Etch removes selected material.

Implantation changes electrical properties.

Annealing repairs and activates the implanted regions.

Cleaning prepares surfaces and removes contamination.

CMP periodically flattens the accumulated topography.

Process control checks whether all of this stayed inside specification.

Then the cycle repeats.

This is the mental shift from "a list of semiconductor tools" to "a connected production system."

The value-chain map today

Uneven patterned wafer

↓

CMP slurry chemically reacts with the target surface

↓

Pad + controlled pressure mechanically remove material

↓

Endpoint and profile control stop at the intended level

↓

Post-CMP clean and dry remove slurry, particles and residue

↓

Inspection/metrology confirm planarity and defects

↓

Flat wafer returns to deposition/lithography/etch for the next layer

Remember these 7 things

1. CMP repeatedly flattens the wafer so later lithography and multilayer construction can remain precise.

2. It combines chemistry with mechanical polishing rather than simply grinding material away.

3. Endpoint control is crucial because under-polishing leaves unwanted material and over-polishing damages useful structures.

4. CMP is both an equipment business and a recurring consumables business through slurry, pads and related materials.

5. Applied Materials and EBARA are the two major CMP equipment names to remember; EBARA reports the No. 2 global position and more than 4,000 cumulative CMP system shipments.

6. CMP is indispensable but is not automatically the industry's biggest bottleneck; distinguish essential processes from genuinely scarce capacity.

7. Start translating macro demand into fab demand with: wafer starts × process steps ÷ effective tool throughput = rough tool-capacity requirement.

Quick check

Why can't a fab simply deposit each new layer on top of the uneven surface created by the previous layer?

Answer: topography would compound from layer to layer, making later films and especially lithography increasingly difficult to control. CMP periodically resets the surface so the next layer can be built with nanometer-scale precision.

Next lesson

